# =============================================================================
# Sainted Word Records — environment variables (example)
# Copy to .env and fill in real values. NEVER commit .env.
# =============================================================================

# ----- M1: Auth + storage -----

# Where the JSON store + uploaded blobs live (dev/local). Default: ./data
# ONLY used when DATABASE_URL is unset (see below). In Vercel prod this
# would be the per-instance /tmp, which does not survive a cold start.
SWRC_DATA_DIR=./data

# ----- M1: Auth + project store: Postgres (REQUIRED in production) -----
#
# Users, sessions, magic-link verifications and projects are all stored
# here. Without DATABASE_URL they fall back to the filesystem, which on
# Vercel is a per-instance /tmp that dies with the instance -- a cold
# start silently wipes every sign-in and saved project (symptom: sign in,
# then /api/manifest?action=health reports users: 0).
#
# Any Postgres works (Neon, Supabase, Vercel Postgres). Provision it from
# the dashboard: Storage -> Create Database -> Postgres -> Connect Project,
# which injects DATABASE_URL for you.
#
# Prefer the provider's POOLED connection string: serverless invocations
# each open their own connection, and the pooler multiplexes them.
#
# Local development (no Postgres needed -- ./data persists on disk):
#   docker run -d --name swr-pg-dev \
#     -e POSTGRES_USER=swr -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=swrdev -e POSTGRES_DB=swr \
#     -p 5433:5432 postgres:16-alpine
#   DATABASE_URL='postgres://swr:swrdev@localhost:5433/swr?sslmode=disable' \
#     npm run check:db-postgres
#
# The table is created on first use; no migration step to run.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/db

# ----- Storage backend: Vercel Blob -----
# Uploaded blobs (images / audio / clips) persist across cold starts when
# EITHER credential set resolves:
#
#   a) BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN                       (per-store token)
#   b) BLOB_STORE_ID + VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN            (OIDC)
#
# Both are set up from the dashboard:
#   Storage → Create Database → Blob → then "Connect Project" on the
#   store. Tick "Add a read-write token env var" to get (a); leaving it
#   unticked gives (b) only, with VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN injected by the
#   platform per invocation.
#
# Either works — the storage layer detects both. Confirm which is live:
#   curl 'https://<host>/api/manifest?action=health'   →  .storage.mode
#   'local-fs' means uploads will NOT survive a cold start.
#
# When NEITHER is set (local `npm run dev` without a Vercel account): the
# storage layer falls back to $SWRC_DATA_DIR/storage/<userId>/. Uploads
# persist for the life of the dev process only.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# BLOB_STORE_ID=

# Public origin of the deployed app. Used to build magic-link URLs when
# the request host header isn't trustworthy (rare).
SWRC_APP_ORIGIN=https://sainted-word-records.vercel.app

# ----- M1: Magic-link email (optional in dev) -----
# If unset, magic links print to stdout (visible in `npm run dev` console).
# Three transports are supported, picked at call time by api/_lib/email.js:
#
#   1. SMTP (any provider — Mailgun, SES SMTP, Postmark SMTP, Postfix, etc.)
#      Set SMTP_HOST to enable. Takes precedence over Resend.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587                  # 587 = STARTTLS; 465 = implicit TLS (secure:true)
# SMTP_USER=apikey               # optional for open relays
# SMTP_PASS=secret               # optional for open relays
# SMTP_SECURE=false              # explicit override; defaults to (port === 465)
#
#   2. Resend (HTTPS API; takes precedence over stdout only when SMTP not set)
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxx
#
#   3. stdout (default if neither transport is configured)
#
# Common:
# SWR_FROM_EMAIL=SWR <noreply@saintedwordrecords.com>

# ----- The store: Gumroad downloads + Stripe Payment Links -----
#
# The catalogue lives in site-map.json → `store`:
#   store.gumroad            the Gumroad storefront
#   store.downloads[]        the products sold as downloads (source dirs,
#                            price, gumroadUrl, downloadUrl)
#   store.products[]         the Stripe products (price, surface, stripeUrl)
#
# The pages read that block (lib/store.client.js), so adding a product to
# site-map.json puts it on the marketplace with no HTML edit. These env
# vars are OPTIONAL build-time overrides for a deployment that would
# rather not commit the links — they win over the file:
#
#   GUMROAD_STORE_URL   the store URL
#   GUMROAD_LINKS       JSON { productId: gumroadProductUrl }
#   STRIPE_LINKS        JSON { productId: stripePaymentLink }
#
# A product with no link renders as "Coming soon" / a disabled button —
# never as a dead checkout URL. So the site is safe to deploy before the
# links exist.
#
# GUMROAD_STORE_URL=https://kaidjuric.gumroad.com
# GUMROAD_LINKS={"preset-pack":"https://kaidjuric.gumroad.com/l/preset-pack"}
# STRIPE_LINKS={"logo-tee":"https://buy.stripe.com/xxxxxxxx","swr-single":"https://buy.stripe.com/yyyyyyyy"}
#
# ----- Off-site hosting for the download products -----
#
# `npm run store:build` packages each store.downloads[].source into
# out/store/<id>.zip — outside dist/, so the product payloads never ship
# with the site. `npm run store:publish` pushes those archives off-site
# and writes the public URL back into store.downloads[].downloadUrl.
#
# The host is the first one with a credential:
#   BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN   Vercel Blob (first-party to this deploy; the
#                           same token the user uploads already use)
#   HF_TOKEN + HF_REPO      the Hugging Face Hub (free, cold archives; a
#                           dataset repo, pushed with the `hf` CLI)
# With neither set the publish script prints "(env skip: ...)" and exits 0.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# HF_TOKEN=
# HF_REPO=kaidjuric/swr-products

# ----- The shipped media off-site: the curated packs -----
#
# `packs/` is 582 files and 39.6 MB — a quarter of the build. The packs
# are opt-in content, and the only thing the deploy needs to serve them
# is packs/manifest.json. Set PACKS_BASE to an absolute URL and the build
# ships ONLY the manifest, with its `base` rewritten to that URL: the
# media is fetched cross-origin from the host and never enters the build.
#
# `npm run media:publish` uploads the packs to the host and prints the
# PACKS_BASE line to paste here (and into the deploy's environment).
# The host must serve `access-control-allow-origin`, or the engine's
# library cannot fetch the media — the publish script checks this and
# fails if the header is missing.
#
# Unset (the default): the whole packs directory ships with the build,
# exactly as before. Nothing changes until this is set.
#
# PACKS_BASE=https://<store>.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/packs/

# ----- M2/M3 -----
# (DATABASE_URL moved to the M1 section above -- it is required in prod.)
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=xxx                                     # M2
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=xxx                                  # M2
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=xxx                              # M2
# R2_BUCKET=swr-user-content                             # M2
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxx                          # M2
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_xxx                        # M2

# ----- Google OAuth2 (sign-in + Drive import) -----
# Required for "Continue with Google" on /auth/login (sign-in) and for the
# "Connect Google Drive" button on the engine (api/auth/google.js).
#
# Two flows, one client:
#   signin — /api/auth/google?intent=signin sends the visitor to Google with
#            the identity scopes only (openid email profile). Google comes
#            back to /api/auth/google/callback, the server resolves the
#            Google identity to a SWR account, starts a session and sets the
#            swrc_session cookie. No Drive access is requested.
#   drive  — /api/auth/google?intent=drive adds drive.readonly, stores the
#            tokens on user.googleDrive and answers the opener popup
#            (lib/google-drive.client.js).
#
# The state that carries the return path and the flow mode is HMAC-signed
# with the client secret, and the return path is restricted to this site.
# When these vars are unset, /api/auth/google answers 503
# google_oauth_not_configured.
#
# Setup (one-time, 5-10 min):
#   1. Open https://console.cloud.google.com
#   2. New project (or pick an existing one). Note the project number.
#   3. APIs & Services → Library → search "Google Drive API" → Enable
#   4. APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen
#        - User type: External
#        - Scopes: .../auth/userinfo.email, .../auth/userinfo.profile,
#          openid, AND https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.readonly
#          (the sign-in only asks for openid/email/profile; Drive is
#          requested by the Drive-link flow alone)
#        - Add the test user emails you want to allow (no need to ship
#          for verification while still in Testing mode)
#   5. APIs & Services → Credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID
#        - Application type: Web application
#        - Name: anything (e.g. "SWR dev" / "SWR production")
#        - Authorized JavaScript origins:
#            http://localhost:5174
#            https://sainted-word-records.vercel.app
#        - Authorized redirect URIs (must match GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI exactly):
#            http://localhost:5174/api/auth/google/callback
#            https://sainted-word-records.vercel.app/api/auth/google/callback
#        - Create. The modal shows Client ID + Client secret. Save both.
#   6. (Optional, recommended for production) Verify the app:
#        OAuth consent screen → Publishing status → Submit for verification.
#        Without this, only test users can link Drive; everyone else sees
#        "Google hasn't verified this app" — annoying but not broken.
#
# Note: the redirect URI must match GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI byte-for-byte.
# A trailing slash or a different scheme fails with redirect_uri_mismatch.
#
# Required variables (set all three in production; the SECRET must be
# marked sensitive so the Vercel dashboard redacts its value):
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID      OAuth 2.0 client ID, e.g. xxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET  OAuth 2.0 client secret, e.g. GOCSPX-...
#   GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI   Full URL of /api/auth/google/callback
#                         (must match the URI registered in Google Cloud Console)
#
# Production setup (run these from your local machine — the Vercel CLI
# requires network access to api.vercel.com):
#   vercel env add GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID     production --value "xxx.apps.googleusercontent.com" --type config
#   vercel env add GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET production --value "GOCSPX-..."     --type secret --sensitive
#   vercel env add GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI  production --value "https://sainted-word-records.vercel.app/api/auth/google/callback" --type config
#
# Local dev (after creating the credentials in Google Cloud Console):
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-...
#   GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5174/api/auth/google/callback
#
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI is optional locally: without it the redirect URI is
# derived from the request host (http://localhost:5174/api/auth/google/callback).
#
# The /api/drive/{files,import,unlink}.js routes work without these
# (the tokens live in the user's record, and "no tokens" returns a clean
# 401 from each endpoint). What breaks without these vars is the OAuth
# round-trip itself — the user sees the Google consent screen redirect
# with redirect_uri_mismatch.
#
# Side effect: this feature does NOT need a Google service account or
# the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS env var. Server-side Drive access
# uses the user's OAuth tokens (offline access_type). Server-to-server
# service accounts are a different product feature and would land in
# a separate config block below.

# ----- M2: Stripe Connect marketplace pilot -----
#
# Account type on stripe.com must be "Platforms and marketplaces"
# (Stripe Connect) — "Products or services" alone cannot onboard connected
# accounts. If direct sales (PT licenses, render service) stay live too,
# pick "Both" so the Payments capability is kept.
#
# Each joining seller gets their own connected account (type: express) via
# /api/connect, then finishes identity + payout-bank verification on a
# Stripe-hosted onboarding page. Signed events land on /api/webhooks/stripe.
#
# Connect data lives in db.js's own store (Vercel Postgres when
# DATABASE_URL is set — the pilot's home; JSON files in dev). Google
# Firestore is a DORMANT alternative: it stays off unless you opt in with
# SWR_CONNECT_STORE=firestore (which also requires `npm install
# firebase-admin` and the FIREBASE_* vars below).
#
# STRIPE_PLATFORM_FEE_PERCENT=10        # buyer-side service fee at checkout (default 10; creators always keep 100%)
# SWR_CONNECT_PILOT_LIMIT=20            # first-tranche cap (default 20; raise to grow)
# SWR_CONNECT_STORE=firestore           # OPT-IN only: switch connect data to Firestore
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=swr-marketplace   # required when SWR_CONNECT_STORE=firestore
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT={"type":"service_account",...}   # JSON string, or:
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/path/to/service-account.json
# LIVEBLOCKS_SECRET_KEY=sk_dev_xxx                       # M3
